// Becoming an interface object must not launder region storage. Found during A9; OLDER than A9,
// and it was a live use-after-free.
//
// **This program has no generics in it.** It was measured accepted on a pristine v0.0.276 —
// printing `secret-value`, then garbage once the region's bytes were handed out again — while
// `kept = b.get()` on the same method and `kept = b.s` on the same field were both REFUSED. The
// only difference is the hop through the interface object.
//
// The taint died at the COERCION, not at the call. `let h: dynamic Holder = b;` asked
// `expr_allocates` about a `DynCoerce`, that node had no arm, the `_` at the bottom of the match
// answered `false`, and `h` was never put in `region_locals`. Every rule downstream then asked a
// correctly implemented question about a value it had been told was safe — which is why the dyn
// CALL arm looked right in review: it is right, and it was being fed a lie.
//
// An interface object BORROWS the storage of the value it refers to — the compiler says exactly
// that when it insists the source be a variable — so it is region storage precisely when that
// variable is. One arm, and it is B33's and B34's shape a third time.
//
// **What did not catch it, which is the part worth keeping.** Both compilers' suites, the
// self-hosting fixpoint and the 133-program corpus were all green with this open. The corpus
// scored 0 divergences because not one of its programs relays region storage out through an
// interface object. A sweep that reports 100% has stopped being evidence; the probe is the check.
interface Holder { function get(self) -> String }
class Box { s: String }
implement Holder for Box {
    function (self) get() -> String { return self.s; }
}

let mutable kept: String = "";
region r {
    let b: Box = Box { s: "secret-" + "value" };
    let h: dynamic Holder = b;
    kept = h.get();
}
print(kept);
